[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1697","stub":{"id":"1697","section":"1. SC Route 14, Pelham, to SC Route 296","altname":"","river":"Enoree","difficulty":"IItoIII","states":["USA-SCR"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"3.98","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"111071","location":{"latitude":"34.8661","longitude":"-82.2353"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"116338","location":{"latitude":"34.8247","longitude":"-82.1892"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"4","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"101248442","title":null,"post_date":"2011-07-25 04:00:00+00","detail":"Erik Olsen shared:\nEnoree River, Greer, SC. 7-16-11 water level 4.1\n\nThere are now two cross-stream strainer trees before the first rapid. At low water we were able to go under the main trunk; at higher water level, they could be a problem.\n\nWe encountered several other strainers that had been trimmed back for floaters.\n\nMost of the drops had to be examined to find the main water flow point in order to float down; otherwise there were a rock slide.\n\nThere is a single rope swing, river right, approx. half way down this section at the end of a nice rapid / drop.\n\nThe good \"chute\" rapid was doable at this level. It is possible to portage this on left or on the right.\n\nOne of our party made it through the chute but flipped at the bottom and had a wet exit and almost lost the paddle.\n\nThere are 2 rope swings: 1 river rt and one river left, at the confluence of Abner Creek and Enoree. This marks the first possible take out a trail on river right at top of the high bank, access by going up Abner creek 10-20 feet.\n\nMaybe 100 -200 yards down river from the rope swings is another takeout marked by a huge strainer and tree in the middle of the river. This is where we took out.\n\nTotal run time for us was 3 hours because we portaged and watched swimmers on rope swings for a while.\n\nShuttle is just 20 min. each way from Hwy 14 down to the take out in the Mahaffey Subdivision which is before the 296 bridge.","user":{"name":"Matt Muir"}}],"photos":[{"id":"VPGE5pa868TVW48voSGrE","ext":"jpg","post_id":"ea8lUQWdgVKU6WFyhBZ9F","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Erik Teriele","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-06-27T14:57:25.000Z","updated_at":"2025-07-02T14:57:25.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/VPGE5pa868TVW48voSGrE","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/VPGE5pa868TVW48voSGrE"}},{"id":"XR3nMr9ROAItZ3vjagNwO","ext":"jpg","post_id":"ea8lUQWdgVKU6WFyhBZ9F","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Erik Teriele","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-06-27T14:57:26.000Z","updated_at":"2025-07-02T14:57:26.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/XR3nMr9ROAItZ3vjagNwO","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/XR3nMr9ROAItZ3vjagNwO"}},{"id":"CJGyavtztzfme8mTPMVZW","ext":"jpg","post_id":"ea8lUQWdgVKU6WFyhBZ9F","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Erik Teriele","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-06-27T14:57:26.000Z","updated_at":"2025-07-02T14:57:26.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/CJGyavtztzfme8mTPMVZW","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/CJGyavtztzfme8mTPMVZW"}},{"id":"rNJZ6KXOx2SvBF7EVDpff","ext":"jpg","post_id":"ea8lUQWdgVKU6WFyhBZ9F","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Erik Teriele","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-06-27T14:57:26.000Z","updated_at":"2025-07-02T14:57:26.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/rNJZ6KXOx2SvBF7EVDpff","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/rNJZ6KXOx2SvBF7EVDpff"}},{"id":"OhWLMJTfCtb99OrTb7yHL","ext":"jpg","post_id":"ea8lUQWdgVKU6WFyhBZ9F","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Erik Teriele","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-06-27T14:57:26.000Z","updated_at":"2025-07-02T14:57:26.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/OhWLMJTfCtb99OrTb7yHL","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/OhWLMJTfCtb99OrTb7yHL"}},{"id":"611668","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"611667","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/611668","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/611668"}},{"id":"544030","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"544029","description":"River before final rapid on this section","caption":"Calm before the storm","poi_name":"Over and under","subject":"","author":"Ryan Jones","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/544030","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/544030"}},{"id":"883893","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886600","description":"The first rapids of our trip down the Enoree River. Andrew found his way down without spilling out of his kayak.\r\nPhoto by: WillPaul","caption":"Enoree River/ Andrew","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Will McDonald","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883893","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883893"}},{"id":"883892","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886599","description":"Putting in the Enoree River early in the morning. Luke (far back) was sleeping by the end. \r\nPhoto by: WillPaul","caption":"Putting In ","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Will McDonald","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883892","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883892"}},{"id":"883886","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886594","description":"We put in at the Hwy 14 bridge on the right side of 14. The river was a little low that day because it was mid June and hadn't rained for a while. Overall the river was nice and the rapids were still on point. At the end we did a takout at the 296 bridge (it was marked by orange tape on the left side of the river courtesy of Cody's Kayaks). ","caption":"Enoree River Kayaking","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883886","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883886"}},{"id":"385055","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"385052","description":"low water 3.5 on online gauge .last rapid befor the 296 climb out.lots of trees down most you could get over or under but a few you did have to portage \r\n  Also at the 296 bridge on left when you climb out and get to the leval area there is a under ground Yellow jackets nest they were very friendly and bit the #$%$ out of me so be carefull ....","caption":"low water","poi_name":"","subject":"Don Frank","author":"Ryan","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/385055","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/385055"}},{"id":"886833","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"889599","description":"If you want  to walk a little level, sandy creek bed instead of climb a steep, brushy bank, try taking this Vines (Abner) creek route on river-left between 2 rope swings if the water isn't absolutely roaring. It's about 400 paces up the creek to Bennetts Bridge Rd. where there's a brush-free exit creek-left just past the bridge. Parking seems fine there too.  ","caption":"Takeout via Abner Creek?","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Glenn Atkisson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/886833","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/886833"}},{"id":"544016","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"544011","description":"b5smmu  <a href=\"http://ekxfexlezcgv.com/\">ekxfexlezcgv</a>, [url=http://hijaprvwrdxz.com/]hijaprvwrdxz[/url], [link=http://cwxamoskkpbv.com/]cwxamoskkpbv[/link], http://hqxkcsxxlese.com/","caption":"VYRXJ6","poi_name":"Over and under","subject":"","author":"Ryan Jones","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/544016","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/544016"}},{"id":"544026","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"544025","description":"First Rapid on this section of enoree","caption":"Don't go over sideways..","poi_name":"The Dam","subject":"","author":"Ryan Jones","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/544026","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/544026"}},{"id":"611675","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"611667","description":"enoree at 10.5 ft","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/611675","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/611675"}},{"id":"612851","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"612846","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Ryan Jones","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/612851","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/612851"}},{"id":"613009","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"612846","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Ryan Jones","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/613009","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/613009"}},{"id":"874191","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"874192","description":"Great beginner run when it is between 5 to 6'","caption":"Enoree WW Trainees","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/874191","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/874191"}},{"id":"613029","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"612846","description":"next rapid after pelham falls","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Ryan Jones","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/613029","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/613029"}},{"id":"611671","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"611667","description":"enoree at 10.5 ft","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/611671","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/611671"}},{"id":"883894","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886601","description":"The third rapids on our trip prooved to be the hardest to navigate. \r\n\r\nPhoto by: WillPaul","caption":"Enoree River/ James","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Will McDonald","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883894","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883894"}},{"id":"340845","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"340846","description":"low water ","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/340845","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/340845"}}],"detail":{"id":"1697","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Takeout below the confluence with Abner Creek.\n\nTodd Corey shared:\n\nI ran this section on 4/24 with my 14- and 11-yr-old. We put on at 2 pm and took off at 5 pm. The river was running at 5.5' and was a great level for an entry level kayaker. The kids walked the dam and the last rapid. Other than that, everything was good to go. There are a few places to play as well, so take your playboat with you. Just don't spend too much time underwater since it isn't the cleanest whitewater you can find. The people walking the trail along river right of the river enjoyed the show. It also makes it easy for you to get out and hike back up to the top of a rapid in case you want to run it again. This is primarily class II with a couple of class III's thrown in. We enjoyed the trip and will head back again given the right water levels.\n\nAnother commenter:\n\nI've paddled this section three times. At below 4 ft it's very rocky in spots. At almost 5ft it's a good paddle with some great little spots especially the spot in one of the pics....great playspot. I would recommend running this at 5 or 6 ft for a great ride...the takeout at 296 is no a fun hike with a kayak...beware.....we took out there and my friend got attacked by yellow jackets....there is a nest if you take out river left be careful and stay on the path.\n\nJim Spears contributed:\n\nI have only paddled this section once several years ago. It is in a fairly developed area now. On river right for the first mile or so there are large houses. This section has some nice small whitewater features. The problem is that the takeout at the first bridge Hwy. 296 (Reidville Rd.) was real grown up. We opted to paddle the next section also from Hwy. 296 to Hwy. 417. That was a mistake. There were an incredible number of riverwide deadfalls on that section. We ended up pushing our canoes down the river and lifting them over the obtacles. The upper section, however, is something to look at for easy whitewater that is very close to I-85 and Greenville-Spartanburg. This would be a good trip for beginner paddlers at a good water level.","averageGradient":"11","maxGradient":"28","length":"4.9","correlations":[{"reachID":"1697","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","belowRecommendedRangeComment":"For flow recommendations, see the comments tab.","lowRunnableRangeComment":"For flow recommendations, see the comments tab.","mediumRunnableRangeComment":"For flow recommendations, see the comments tab.","highRunnableRangeComment":"For flow recommendations, see the comments tab.","aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"4","beginMediumRunnable":"5","beginHighRunnable":"6","endHighRunnable":"8"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"ENOREE RIVER AT PELHAM, SC","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"02160326","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"55","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"3.98","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"34.856575","longitude":"-82.22581389"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/02160326","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/02160326/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"3.98","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111071","116338"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-82.23579491960824,34.86625249287452],[-82.23583031612719,34.866137612547504],[-82.23583031612719,34.86602221254765],[-82.2357567161273,34.86569787921485],